.docname {The interface}
.include {docs}

The window has four parts: the toolbar at the top, the pattern palette on the left, the board canvas in the middle, and the status bar at the bottom. All of them are built with native Bevy UI.

## Toolbar

The toolbar is a single 64-pixel row at every window size. It has three groups, with a subtle divider before the board edits:

- **Playback:** Play / Pause, Step, and the speed field, whose last stop past 40 gen/s is ∞, maximum speed (see [Playback and rules](simulation.qd#speed))
- **Board edits:** Randomize, Clear, Undo, and Redo; history buttons are disabled when unavailable
- **Workspace:** the **Grid**, **Cell age**, and **Pattern palette** toggles, Import / Export, **Settings** (a gear), and **Help** (a question mark), at the right end

Below 900 pixels wide, the workspace controls move into a menu behind a **More** button (**⋯**) at the right end, where each one is labelled. Choosing an item, clicking outside the menu, or pressing .keybinding {Escape} closes it, and the click that closes it does not draw on the board. From the keyboard, the menu opens with its first item focused. Tab order follows the layout: the toolbar, the tools, the palette,
the status bar, and last the board, which is outlined when it has keyboard focus.
Resizing retains the focused control, camera pan, and zoom relative to the fitted board.

Most buttons show icons instead of words. Play turns into a pause icon while the simulation runs. The Grid, Cell age, and Pattern palette toggles have a green outline while they are on. Hover over or focus a button to see its name and shortcut. Screen readers announce icon buttons by name, and the toggles as on or off.

## Pattern palette

The palette holds the editing tools and the pattern library, from top to bottom:

- the **Draw**, **Erase**, and **Stamp** tools
- a slim **search field** that filters patterns by name, ignoring case
- a **stamp preview** of the selected pattern in its current orientation, with **Rotate** and **Flip** buttons, shown while a stamp is active. It appears under the search field, so choosing a stamp never moves the field
- the number of matching patterns
- pattern **thumbnails**, with built-in and imported patterns in separate groups

Only the thumbnails scroll, with the mouse wheel or the scrollbar; the tools, search, and stamp preview stay in place. Filtering the library keeps the current stamp selected, even if the filter hides it, and scrolls back to the first match.

Press .keybinding {H} or click **Palette** to hide or show the palette. Hiding it gives the board nearly the full width of the window: the tools become a slim rail of icons (a pencil, an eraser, and a stamp) along the left edge, whose tooltips name them. See [Drawing and stamps](editing.qd) for how the tools work.

## Board canvas

The board has its own canvas beside the palette and between the toolbar and status bar. Drawing and stamp previews stay inside it, including on Retina displays.

| Action                         | How                                                       |
|--------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| Zoom                           | Scroll over the canvas, or use **−** and **+** beside the zoom percentage in the status bar |
| Pan                            | Middle-button drag, or .keybinding {W} .keybinding {A} .keybinding {S} .keybinding {D} or the arrow keys |
| Fit the whole board            | .keybinding {V}, or **Fit board** (the corner brackets) in the status bar |

The canvas leaves only a thin gap around the board, and a fitted board leaves only 1% of the canvas free on each side in the direction it fills, just enough to keep the fixed-edge walls visible. [Fill canvas](settings.qd#fill-canvas), on by default, sizes the board to the canvas so there is no border on either axis.

The board is fitted into the canvas on startup, after a board size change, and when you press .keybinding {V} or click **Fit board**. When the window size changes or the palette is shown or hidden, the view keeps its pan position and its zoom relative to the fitted view.

The zoom percentage is relative to **Fit board** (100%). Middle-button panning must start on the canvas and stops when the pointer enters a control or leaves the window.

With **fixed** edges, a pale wall outlines the board and pulses briefly when you switch to that mode. With **wrapping** edges, the wall is hidden.

### Grid

Press .keybinding {T} or click **Grid** to draw lines between cells. The grid is drawn only where it is visible, and is skipped when cells are only a few pixels wide, so it costs nothing when zoomed out.

### Cell age

With **Cell age** on, which is the default, the board shows how cells change:

- A cell born in the latest generation is brightest, and settles into the usual live colour over a few generations.
- A cell that has stayed alive for a long time darkens to a deeper shade, so still lifes stand out from busy regions.
- A cell that just died leaves a faint trail that fades over about six generations, so you can see where gliders and other spaceships have been.

Cells you draw, stamp, or restore with undo show the plain live colour, and erased cells leave no trail. Switch it off with the **Cell age** toggle in the toolbar, or in [Settings](settings.qd#display), to see plain live and dead cells.

## Status bar

The status bar reads, for example:

```text
Running   Gen 1520   842 alive −12   B3/S23   (40, 29)
```

From left to right:

- **Running** or **Paused**, in a pill that turns green while the simulation runs.
- The generation counter.
- The number of living cells, and how the latest generation changed it: `+12`, `−12`, or `±0` for a stable population. The change disappears after an edit, undo, or counter reset, since it no longer describes the board.
- The rule. Click it to open [Settings](settings.qd#rule) with the rulestring ready to edit.
- The cell under the pointer, as `(x, y)` counted from the top-left cell `(0, 0)`, while the pointer is over the board.

From 960 pixels wide, the status bar also shows the board size and edge mode. Numbers use tabular digits and keep the widest size they have reached, so the readouts stay close together without shifting as digits change. Clearing or resizing the board, or resetting the generation counter, lets them shrink back to fit. The zoom controls sit at the right end. The status bar is a single 48-pixel row at every window size.

Clear, randomize, undo, and redo show a notice at the bottom left of the canvas for two seconds. Undoable actions include the **U** shortcut. These notices do not intercept drawing or panning.

## Dialogs

| Dialog              | Opens with                               | Contains                                                       |
|---------------------|------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Import / Export** | the **Import / Export** button           | RLE import, and copying the board as RLE                       |
| **Settings**        | the **Settings** button, or the rule in the status bar | board size, rule, theme, grid, cell age, edges, diagnostics, generation reset |
| **Help**            | .keybinding {/} or the **Help** button   | a summary and the full list of shortcuts                       |
| **Diagnostics**     | .keybinding {F3} or **Settings**         | live simulation statistics                                     |

Dialogs are modal. While one is open, typing and scrolling inside it do not reach the board, and board shortcuts are disabled. Press .keybinding {Esc} or click **Close** to close a dialog; focus returns to the control that opened it.

### Diagnostics

The diagnostics dialog shows live statistics while playback continues:

- board size, generation, living cells, and rule
- average step time, as a moving average
- tick interval, or *max speed* (∞)
- measured rate, in generations per second
- the number of timed steps
- the dense fallback threshold (see [Simulation engine](engine.qd#frontier-and-dense-stepping))
